<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Worker">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <SignAssembly>false</SignAssembly>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);MA0004</NoWarn>
    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
    <TrimMode>full</TrimMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" />
    <!--
      Named, although Serilog.Extensions.Hosting brings it, because this project does not pin
      transitively: left to that package it resolves 4.3.0, whose assembly fails this fully trimmed
      publish with IL2104. The central version is trim-clean.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Extensions.Hosting" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Sinks.Console" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="Quartz" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    This is the repository's trim canary: a fully trimmed application over Quartz and nothing else, so
    every IL2xxx it reports is Quartz's own. It used to NoWarn the whole IL2xxx family, which meant the
    leg proved the examples publish rather than that Quartz survives trimming (issue #3341, step 1).

    Nothing is suppressed at all any more. It applied Quartz's ILLink.Suppressions.xml until #4082 took
    the last two entries, and since the whole of Quartz is rooted above, any warning against it now fails
    this publish.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz\Quartz.csproj" />
    <!--
      The dashboard agent, so that this worker can be put on Quartz.Examples.AspNetCore's dashboard
      (Program.cs, Dashboard:AgentEndpoint). It widens what the trimmed publish measures by the agent
      package and the SignalR client under it: the agent records no warning, and the SignalR client is
      trim-annotated by ASP.NET Core.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.Dashboard.Agent\Quartz.Dashboard.Agent.csproj" />
    <!--
      What a consumer gets from analyzers/dotnet/cs inside Quartz.nupkg. A ProjectReference does not
      carry analyzers the way a PackageReference does, so this example - which declares its job with
      [QuartzJob] and calls the AddDeclaredJobs the generator writes - has to name the project.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.Analyzers\Quartz.Analyzers.csproj" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" OutputItemType="Analyzer" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    What buildTransitive/net10.0 inside Quartz.nupkg gives a package consumer, which a ProjectReference
    does not. Load-bearing for this publish: it sets the three feature switches false under PublishTrimmed,
    and ILLink substitutes a guarded call site away only on an explicit setting. Without the import the
    guarded IL2026s are reported against Quartz, and with ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors on, this publish fails.
  -->
  <Import Project="..\Quartz\buildTransitive\net10.0\Quartz.targets" />

</Project>
